{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Python comparison — ROML model-build benchmark{% endblock %}
{% block body %}
<h1>ROML Python vs PuLP / Pyomo / PyOptInterface</h1>
<div class="notice">
<strong>Two panels, two questions.</strong> Section A compares
<strong>formulation</strong>: each arm derives rows and objective from the
same high-level inputs (N, or B/T/prices) during timing. Section B compares
<strong>matrix ingestion</strong>: each arm consumes the same preassembled
CSR matrix, bounds, and objective vector. PyOptInterface's model is
solver-backed — its calls insert directly into HiGHS — while the others
build a separate modeling representation first. PuLP and Pyomo expose no
matrix API and appear in panel A only, by design.
</div>
{% for title, div in charts %}
<div class="chart"><h2>{{ title }}</h2>{{ div | safe }}</div>
{% endfor %}
<h2>Construction paths</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ROML Python (vectorized)</strong> — {{ impl_notes['roml_python_vectorized'] }}</li>
<li><strong>ROML Python (naive chain)</strong> — {{ impl_notes['roml_python_naive_chain'] }}</li>
<li><strong>ROML Python (CSR ingest)</strong> — {{ impl_notes['roml_python_csr'] }}</li>
<li><strong>PuLP</strong> — {{ impl_notes['pulp_python'] }}</li>
<li><strong>Pyomo</strong> — {{ impl_notes['pyomo_python'] }}</li>
<li><strong>PyOptInterface (matrix)</strong> — {{ impl_notes['pyoptinterface_python'] }}</li>
<li><strong>PyOptInterface (scalar)</strong> — {{ impl_notes['pyoptinterface_scalar'] }}</li>
</ul>
<h2>Ingestion diagnostics</h2>
<p>The canonical/shuffled/duplicated CSR charts are diagnostics for the
ingestion path (shuffled defeats sorted-input assumptions; duplicated
exercises duplicate accumulation at 2x nnz). They are not leaderboards.</p>
{% endblock %}
